1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,279
You set off fast.

2
00:00:01,279 --> 00:00:06,649
4 minutes later, you're bent over, gasping,
wondering how anyone runs for an hour.

3
00:00:07,149 --> 00:00:09,541
The problem usually isn't your lungs.

4
00:00:09,541 --> 00:00:12,184
It's a few mistakes you can fix this week.

5
00:00:12,534 --> 00:00:17,505
Today: the 5 mistakes killing your running
stamina, and the fix for each one.

6
00:00:17,855 --> 00:00:20,884
Mistake 1: running every run too fast.

7
00:00:20,884 --> 00:00:23,292
Mistake 2: too much, too soon.

8
00:00:23,292 --> 00:00:25,234
Mistake 3: overstriding.

9
00:00:25,234 --> 00:00:27,486
Mistake 4: no recovery days.

10
00:00:27,486 --> 00:00:29,972
Mistake 5: running underfuelled.

11
00:00:30,322 --> 00:00:32,712
The first one is why you gasp early.

12
00:00:32,712 --> 00:00:35,037
The rest are why you stop improving.

13
00:00:35,387 --> 00:00:37,377
Your body has a redline speed.

14
00:00:37,377 --> 00:00:42,000
Below it, your breathing settles and you
can keep going for a long time.

15
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:45,557
Above it, you're spending a
small, fixed reserve.

16
00:00:45,557 --> 00:00:49,213
The further over the line you go,
the faster it empties.

17
00:00:49,213 --> 00:00:52,035
When it's gone, you have to
slow right down.

18
00:00:52,385 --> 00:00:56,595
Think of your phone on full
brightness with every app open.

19
00:00:56,595 --> 00:00:58,068
Fine for 10 minutes.

20
00:00:58,068 --> 00:00:59,121
Dead by lunch.

21
00:00:59,121 --> 00:01:01,366
Easy pace is battery-saver mode.

22
00:01:01,717 --> 00:01:07,793
And the best endurance athletes do most of their
training easy: around 70 to 80% of

23
00:01:07,793 --> 00:01:08,010
it.

24
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,122
The fix is the talk test.

25
00:01:10,122 --> 00:01:13,374
If you can speak comfortably
in full sentences,

26
00:01:13,374 --> 00:01:19,133
you're at or below your first breathing
threshold, which is well under your redline.

27
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:22,995
If you can only get out 2 or 3
words, slow down, or walk.

28
00:01:23,345 --> 00:01:26,767
Every 5 minutes or so, say a
sentence out loud.

29
00:01:26,767 --> 00:01:28,550
A line from a song works.

30
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:30,197
New to running?

31
00:01:30,197 --> 00:01:31,331
Use run-walk.

32
00:01:31,331 --> 00:01:35,869
The NHS Couch to 5K plan starts
with 8 one-minute runs,

33
00:01:35,869 --> 00:01:38,948
with 90 seconds of walking in between.

34
00:01:39,298 --> 00:01:42,008
Walking breaks are part of
the official plan.

35
00:01:42,008 --> 00:01:43,245
They're not cheating.

36
00:01:43,595 --> 00:01:45,365
The mistake within the fix?

37
00:01:45,365 --> 00:01:50,105
Easy pace that creeps faster, until you're
racing your mate or your watch.

38
00:01:50,105 --> 00:01:52,128
Let your breathing set the pace.

39
00:01:52,728 --> 00:01:54,826
Your muscles get stronger fast.

40
00:01:54,826 --> 00:01:57,251
Your tendons take longer to catch up.

41
00:01:57,601 --> 00:02:03,314
In one review, beginners got injured more than
twice as often per hour of running as

42
00:02:03,314 --> 00:02:04,726
recreational runners.

43
00:02:05,076 --> 00:02:07,188
And injury stops all progress.

44
00:02:07,538 --> 00:02:09,088
Here's the surprise.

45
00:02:09,088 --> 00:02:14,919
In a study of over 5,000 runners, the weekly
increase wasn't linked to injury.

46
00:02:14,919 --> 00:02:16,690
One sudden long run was.

47
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:23,504
A run 10 to 30% longer than your longest in
the last 30 days came with about 1.

48
00:02:23,504 --> 00:02:26,170
6 times the overuse-injury rate.

49
00:02:26,170 --> 00:02:29,483
More than doubling it came with about 2.

50
00:02:29,483 --> 00:02:29,968
3 times.

51
00:02:30,768 --> 00:02:32,484
Think of building a wall.

52
00:02:32,484 --> 00:02:34,465
Your muscles lay bricks fast.

53
00:02:34,465 --> 00:02:36,379
The cement needs time to set.

54
00:02:36,729 --> 00:02:38,861
So the fix is the spike check.

55
00:02:38,861 --> 00:02:43,470
Before any run, ask: is this longer than
my longest run this month?

56
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:49,673
If it is, make it only a little longer: no more
than about 10%, and less is fine.

57
00:02:49,673 --> 00:02:53,313
That's a sensible ceiling, not a
proven safe limit.

58
00:02:53,663 --> 00:02:58,211
If you're coming back after a break, start
from your current longest run,

59
00:02:58,211 --> 00:02:59,871
not the one you used to do.

60
00:03:00,221 --> 00:03:01,779
Starting from zero?

61
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:05,051
Couch to 5K takes 9 weeks, 3 runs a week,

62
00:03:05,051 --> 00:03:08,712
and you can repeat any week
that felt too hard.

63
00:03:09,062 --> 00:03:10,980
The mistake within the fix?

64
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:16,187
Keeping a sensible weekly total, then
cramming it into one huge weekend run.

65
00:03:16,787 --> 00:03:20,646
Overstriding is reaching your foot far
out in front of your body,

66
00:03:20,646 --> 00:03:23,102
so it lands with your leg almost straight.

67
00:03:23,452 --> 00:03:26,865
It's like pulling the handbrake a
tiny bit on every step.

68
00:03:27,215 --> 00:03:31,238
When runners take slightly quicker,
shorter steps at the same speed,

69
00:03:31,238 --> 00:03:33,978
there's less braking and less
load on the knee.

70
00:03:34,328 --> 00:03:37,989
Is it proven to prevent
injuries or boost stamina?

71
00:03:37,989 --> 00:03:38,635
Not yet.

72
00:03:38,635 --> 00:03:40,215
The evidence is thin.

73
00:03:40,215 --> 00:03:42,584
But it's a small, low-risk tweak.

74
00:03:42,934 --> 00:03:47,961
The fix: on an easy run, count the steps
of one foot for 30 seconds,

75
00:03:47,961 --> 00:03:49,418
then multiply by 4.

76
00:03:49,418 --> 00:03:51,531
That's your steps per minute.

77
00:03:51,881 --> 00:03:52,778
Add 5%.

78
00:03:52,778 --> 00:03:55,019
So 160 becomes 168.

79
00:03:55,019 --> 00:04:00,286
Use a metronome app or a
playlist at that beat.

80
00:04:00,637 --> 00:04:05,492
Run 3 to 5 one-minute chunks at the new
rhythm, in easy runs only,

81
00:04:05,492 --> 00:04:07,086
not your longest run.

82
00:04:07,086 --> 00:04:10,130
Over 2 to 4 weeks, make the chunks longer.

83
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:15,941
If your calves, Achilles or feet get sore, go
back to your normal rhythm for a week.

84
00:04:16,291 --> 00:04:17,642
Keep the same speed.

85
00:04:17,642 --> 00:04:20,408
Quicker, shorter steps, not
faster running.

86
00:04:20,759 --> 00:04:24,365
If your stride is pain-free, you
don't have to change it.

87
00:04:24,365 --> 00:04:27,287
If your knee already hurts,
see a physio first.

88
00:04:27,637 --> 00:04:29,751
The mistake within the fix?

89
00:04:29,751 --> 00:04:32,468
Chasing a magic 180 steps a minute.

90
00:04:32,468 --> 00:04:37,375
Studies change cadence by 5 to 10%
from the runner's own rhythm,

91
00:04:37,375 --> 00:04:39,413
and big jumps feel awkward.

92
00:04:40,013 --> 00:04:41,486
Training is the stress.

93
00:04:41,486 --> 00:04:43,512
Recovery is where you get fitter.

94
00:04:43,861 --> 00:04:47,810
Pile on hard sessions without rest,
and you stop improving.

95
00:04:47,810 --> 00:04:50,837
That can take weeks to months
to recover from.

96
00:04:51,187 --> 00:04:53,307
Think of a phone you never charge.

97
00:04:53,307 --> 00:04:56,094
The charging is where the
battery gets bigger.

98
00:04:56,444 --> 00:05:00,274
The fix: no back-to-back run days
when you're starting.

99
00:05:00,274 --> 00:05:03,420
Couch to 5K puts a rest day
between every run.

100
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:09,317
On other days, easy cycling or swimming keeps
your engine ticking with less impact.

101
00:05:09,667 --> 00:05:12,120
And strength train at least 2 days a week.

102
00:05:12,470 --> 00:05:18,020
In a review of sports trials, people who did
strength training ended up with about a

103
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:19,587
third as many injuries.

104
00:05:19,587 --> 00:05:21,154
Stretching didn't help.

105
00:05:21,154 --> 00:05:25,398
Only a few trials tested strength work,
and none were in runners.

106
00:05:25,748 --> 00:05:31,182
Squats, calf raises, glute bridges and
step-ups on a low, stable step.

107
00:05:31,182 --> 00:05:36,692
2 to 3 sets of 8 to 15, stopping with a
couple of reps left in the tank.

108
00:05:37,042 --> 00:05:41,906
Start with body weight, add weight slowly,
and skip any exercise that hurts.

109
00:05:42,256 --> 00:05:43,439
Need more rest?

110
00:05:43,439 --> 00:05:49,426
Watch for easy runs that feel unusually hard
several times in a row, heavy legs,

111
00:05:49,426 --> 00:05:51,643
poor sleep, or frequent colds.

112
00:05:51,993 --> 00:05:55,812
If easing off for a week or two
doesn't fix it, see a doctor.

113
00:05:56,161 --> 00:05:58,028
The mistake within the fix?

114
00:05:58,028 --> 00:06:02,497
Turning your rest day into a hard leg
session or a 5-a-side match.

115
00:06:02,497 --> 00:06:03,564
That isn't rest.

116
00:06:04,164 --> 00:06:06,425
There are two ways to run on empty.

117
00:06:06,775 --> 00:06:10,432
The first is running after
hours without food.

118
00:06:10,432 --> 00:06:16,268
The NHS suggests a light snack 1 to 2 hours
before, like a banana or toast.

119
00:06:16,619 --> 00:06:20,536
For runs under an hour, you don't
need gels or sports drinks.

120
00:06:20,536 --> 00:06:21,547
Water is enough.

121
00:06:21,897 --> 00:06:25,147
On runs longer than about 60
to 90 minutes,

122
00:06:25,147 --> 00:06:28,915
start practising some
carbohydrate during the run,

123
00:06:28,915 --> 00:06:31,796
working towards 30 to 60 grams an hour.

124
00:06:32,145 --> 00:06:37,037
The second is eating too little, day after
day, for the training you do.

125
00:06:37,037 --> 00:06:39,718
Doctors call it low energy availability.

126
00:06:40,069 --> 00:06:44,926
It can block your body's adaptation to
training, so you stop getting fitter.

127
00:06:44,926 --> 00:06:47,386
It affects men and women, at any level.

128
00:06:47,736 --> 00:06:50,029
Think of a car on the reserve light.

129
00:06:50,029 --> 00:06:53,004
It still moves, but it
switches off the extras,

130
00:06:53,004 --> 00:06:56,227
and the extras are the repairs
that make you fitter.

131
00:06:56,577 --> 00:07:00,780
After a run, eat a normal meal with
plenty of carbohydrate.

132
00:07:01,129 --> 00:07:04,512
See a doctor if you're losing
weight without trying,

133
00:07:04,512 --> 00:07:09,172
if your periods stop or become irregular,
or if you keep getting injured.

134
00:07:09,522 --> 00:07:11,497
The mistake within the fix?

135
00:07:11,497 --> 00:07:17,351
Copying marathon fuelling on a 30-minute jog, or
skipping meals while adding miles.

136
00:07:17,951 --> 00:07:22,892
Before you start, talk to a doctor if you have
a heart or lung condition or any

137
00:07:22,892 --> 00:07:23,818
health worries.

138
00:07:24,168 --> 00:07:29,004
If you get chest pain, faint or nearly
faint, or feel unusually breathless,

139
00:07:29,004 --> 00:07:30,277
stop straight away.

140
00:07:30,277 --> 00:07:31,677
Don't push through it.

141
00:07:32,027 --> 00:07:36,818
Call emergency services if chest pain
doesn't go away when you stop,

142
00:07:36,818 --> 00:07:39,804
or spreads to your arm, back, neck or jaw.

143
00:07:39,804 --> 00:07:42,928
Otherwise, see a doctor
before you run again.

144
00:07:43,279 --> 00:07:47,406
Pain in one small spot on a bone that
hurts when you press it,

145
00:07:47,406 --> 00:07:51,663
or pain at rest or at night, needs a
doctor before you run again.

146
00:07:52,013 --> 00:07:54,351
Shin pain that builds as you run?

147
00:07:54,351 --> 00:07:55,864
Don't run through it.

148
00:07:55,864 --> 00:07:59,578
Swap to low-impact exercise and
build back gradually.

149
00:07:59,578 --> 00:08:03,085
If it isn't getting better, see
a doctor or physio.

150
00:08:03,435 --> 00:08:08,126
In hot weather, run in the cool of the
morning, go easier than usual,

151
00:08:08,126 --> 00:08:09,600
and drink cold fluids.

152
00:08:09,950 --> 00:08:15,006
If you feel dizzy or sick, stop running,
get into the shade and cool down.

153
00:08:15,356 --> 00:08:19,469
Call emergency services if you're not
better within 30 minutes,

154
00:08:19,469 --> 00:08:22,426
or straight away if someone
becomes confused,

155
00:08:22,426 --> 00:08:25,639
or their skin is hot but
they've stopped sweating.

156
00:08:25,989 --> 00:08:27,517
Here's a simple week.

157
00:08:27,517 --> 00:08:32,517
If you're on Couch to 5K, its runs go on
Monday, Wednesday and Saturday.

158
00:08:32,867 --> 00:08:34,419
Monday: easy run-walk.

159
00:08:34,419 --> 00:08:35,701
Tuesday: strength.

160
00:08:35,701 --> 00:08:39,075
Wednesday: easy run with a few
quick-feet minutes.

161
00:08:39,425 --> 00:08:42,625
Thursday: strength, or an
easy swim or bike.

162
00:08:42,625 --> 00:08:43,620
Friday: rest.

163
00:08:43,620 --> 00:08:48,954
Saturday: your longest run, the same as
last time or just a little longer.

164
00:08:48,954 --> 00:08:49,878
Sunday: rest.

165
00:08:50,228 --> 00:08:52,402
Play football or another sport?

166
00:08:52,402 --> 00:08:54,644
Count those sessions as run days.

167
00:08:54,994 --> 00:08:58,586
In one small lab study of men
on exercise bikes,

168
00:08:58,586 --> 00:09:01,885
aerobic fitness was already
up after 3 weeks.

169
00:09:02,235 --> 00:09:07,737
Couch to 5K is built to get beginners to 30
minutes of running in about 9 weeks,

170
00:09:07,737 --> 00:09:09,979
and it's fine if it takes longer.

171
00:09:10,329 --> 00:09:13,591
So next time you set off, start
slower than you think.

172
00:09:13,591 --> 00:09:16,260
Talk, don't gasp, and let the
minutes add up.
